---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import cities from '../../data/cities.json';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  const allCities = [
    ...cities.uk.map(c => ({ ...c, country: 'UK' })),
    ...cities.us.map(c => ({ ...c, country: 'US' })),
  ];
  return allCities.map(city => ({
    params: { city: city.slug },
    props: { city },
  }));
}

const { city } = Astro.props;

const title = `Remote Website Audit Services for ${city.name} Businesses`;
const description = `Remote website audits for ${city.name}, ${city.region}. Compare $297 and $997 USD scopes and confirm pages, evidence and deliverables before work begins.`;

const toc = [
  { label: `Remote Service for ${city.name}`, id: 'remote-service' },
  { label: 'What the Audit Can Cover', id: 'covers' },
  { label: `How ${city.name} Changes the Scope`, id: 'local-scope' },
  { label: 'Evidence and Deliverables', id: 'deliverables' },
  { label: 'Pricing', id: 'pricing' },
  { label: 'Before You Enquire', id: 'get-started' },
  { label: 'Other Service Areas', id: 'also-serving' },
];

const allCities = [
  ...cities.uk.map(c => ({ ...c, country: 'UK' })),
  ...cities.us.map(c => ({ ...c, country: 'US' })),
];
const sameRegion = allCities.filter(c => c.region === city.region && c.slug !== city.slug);
const sameCountry = allCities.filter(c => c.country === city.country && c.slug !== city.slug);
const nearbyCities = (sameRegion.length >= 3 ? sameRegion : sameCountry).slice(0, 5);

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'Service',
  name: 'Website Audit Service',
  provider: {
    '@type': 'Organization',
    '@id': 'https://auditweb.site/#organization',
    name: 'AuditWeb',
    url: 'https://auditweb.site/',
  },
  areaServed: ['US', 'GB'],
  description: 'Professional website audits covering SEO, technical, content, UX, and security across an agreed scope.',

};
---

<ContentLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  breadcrumbs={[
    { label: 'Website Audit', href: '/website-audit/' },
    { label: 'Services', href: '/website-audit-services/' },
    { label: city.name, href: `/website-audit-services/${city.slug}/` },
  ]}
  toc={toc}
  schema={schema}
  canonical="https://auditweb.site/website-audit-services/"
  showLeadForm={true}
  publishDate="2026-03-28"
  updateDate="2026-09-11"
>
  <p>AuditWeb provides website audits remotely to organisations based in or targeting {city.name}, {city.region}. AuditWeb does not claim a local office or in-person auditor in {city.name}.</p>
  <p>The city affects the pages and competitors selected for review. It does not change the evidence standard or the USD price shown on the main service.</p>

  <span id="process" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 id="remote-service">How does the remote service work for {city.name}?</h2>
  <p>The remote service starts with a written scope. Share the website, the business question, the important {city.name} pages and any markets outside {city.region} that the review must consider.</p>
  <p>Public pages can be reviewed without an account. Read-only Google Search Console or analytics access can add search and journey evidence when available. Agree access before work starts and do not send passwords through the enquiry form.</p>

  <span id="issues" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 id="covers">What can the website audit cover?</h2>
  <p>The audit can cover technical SEO, on-page content, performance, mobile experience and agreed public security checks. The final scope depends on the question and the page sample.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Technical SEO:</strong> crawl access, response codes, canonical signals, internal links and indexing evidence.</li>
    <li><strong>Content:</strong> titles, descriptions, headings, intent, duplication and content gaps across the agreed pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Performance and mobile:</strong> key templates, loading behaviour and important visitor journeys.</li>
    <li><strong>Security observations:</strong> public HTTPS and response-header checks. Active testing requires explicit authorisation and separate scope.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Google explains that crawling, canonicalisation, sitemaps and JavaScript rendering are separate parts of search access in its <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/crawling-indexing">crawling and indexing documentation</a>. A report should record which part was checked and what evidence supports the finding.</p>

  <span id="why" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <h2 id="local-scope">How does {city.name} change the audit scope?</h2>
  <p>A {city.name} scope changes the comparison set. It can prioritise pages aimed at people in {city.name}, local landing-page duplication, location information and competitors that the client names.</p>
  <p>AuditWeb does not infer local search behaviour from the city name. Supply target services, customer locations and known competitors during scoping. Competitive conclusions require a dated comparison rather than a generic city claim.</p>
  <ul>
    <li>List the {city.name} landing pages or store pages that matter.</li>
    <li>Name the services and search journeys the audit should investigate.</li>
    <li>Identify other regions that share content or templates with {city.name}.</li>
    <li>Provide read-only measurement access when search or conversion evidence is part of the question.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="deliverables">What evidence and deliverables do you receive?</h2>
  <p>The report records the agreed scope, observed evidence, affected URLs, recommended fixes and verification steps. Findings distinguish confirmed problems from questions that need more access or testing.</p>
  <p>Review the <a href="/website-audit-services/report-example/">completed website audit report example</a> before commissioning work. The example shows AuditWeb's own dated evidence and does not present invented client results.</p>

  <h2 id="pricing">How much does a {city.name} website audit cost?</h2>
  <p>Basic audits start at $297 USD. Comprehensive audits start at $997 USD. Large sites, migrations, multiple languages and complex architectures use a custom quote.</p>
  <div class="overflow-x-auto" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label={`${city.name} website audit prices`}>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Scope</th><th>Price</th><th>Suitable use</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>Basic</strong></td><td>$297 USD</td><td>A focused review of a smaller site or a defined question</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Comprehensive</strong></td><td>$997 USD</td><td>A broader technical, content, competitive and experience review</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Custom</strong></td><td>Quoted</td><td>Large sites, migrations or complex architectures</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>Confirm the page sample, deliverables, access, delivery date and any follow-up support in writing. Rankings and revenue outcomes are not guaranteed.</p>

  <h2 id="get-started">What should you prepare before enquiring?</h2>
  <p>Prepare the site URL, the main business question, priority {city.name} pages and any deadlines or access limits. AuditWeb can then confirm an appropriate scope and price.</p>
  <p>Use the <a href="/free-website-audit/">free pasted-HTML checker</a> for an initial markup review. The checker does not crawl a website or measure live speed, security or indexation. Read the <a href="/website-audit-services/">main service page</a> for the full commercial terms.</p>

  <h2 id="also-serving">Which other service areas are listed?</h2>
  <p>AuditWeb also offers the same remote scoping process to organisations in other listed areas. These links describe service availability and do not represent office locations.</p>
  <ul>
    {nearbyCities.map(c => (
      <li><a href={`/website-audit-services/${c.slug}/`}>{c.name}, {c.region}</a></li>
    ))}
  </ul>
</ContentLayout>
